might not need it. The next player has F4, etc. The player across has F4, F3, F2. Maybe they are collecting winds. But J3 is White Dragon. \n\nAlternatively, playing T9. If I play T9, maybe someone else can use it. But T9 is a terminal, so maybe not. \n\nConsidering the Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) and if I can get a Dragon Pung (2 points) by getting another J3. So total 6 points. Need 2 more. Maybe another pattern. \n\nIf I have a pair of J3s, that's a pair. But if I can get a third, that's a pung. \n\nAlternatively, if I play B7, and then later get B8, but the player across has B8 already. So no. \n\nHmm. The best move might be to play J3, keeping the possibility of forming a pung later. Even though it's a risk, it could lead to more points. \n\nSo the answer is Play J3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing J3 allows the possibility of forming a pung of J3 later, contributing to Dragon Pung (2 points) and potentially combining with Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) for 6 points. Other plays like T9 or B7 offer less strategic flexibility.  \nReason: Prioritizing the chance to form a pung of J3 (White Dragon) aligns with scoring patterns and maximizes future point potential.  \nAnswer: Play J3"}
