ase, since the players already have those tiles in their melds, it's safe to play them.\n\nAnother consideration: the current hand has T6 and B6. If I play B6, then I have T6 left. If I play T6, I have B6 left. Which is better? Maybe it's better to play the one that is part of a more common meld. For example, T6 is part of the next player's T6-T7-T8. But they already have it. So no problem. B6 is part of the player across's B6-B7-B8. So same thing.\n\nHmm. It's a bit tricky. Maybe I should play B6, as it's a non-terminal and helps towards All Terminals and Honors. Let's say I play B6. Then my hand would have T2, T6, T8 left as non-terminals. Then I can focus on getting rid of those. Alternatively, if I play T6, I still have B6. Either way, I need to get rid of the remaining non-terminals.\n\nBut the valid actions include Play B6. So maybe that's the best move. Let's check the unshown tiles. B6 has 1 left, so playing it would remove it from the game. Since the player across has B6 in their meld, they can't take it again. So it's safe to play B6. This would help me move towards All Terminals and Honors by removing a non-terminal. So I think playing B6 is a good move.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing B6 is a strategic move to eliminate a non-terminal tile, aiding in the pursuit of an All Terminals and Honors hand.  \nReason: Discarding B6 removes a non-terminal tile, which is critical for forming an All Terminals and Honors hand (16 points). Since the player across has already melded B6-B7-B8, they cannot claim it, making it a safe discard. This action also reduces the number of non-terminal tiles in the hand, aligning with the goal of maximizing terminal/honor tiles.  \nAnswer: Play B6"}
